WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.019 
 This past year, the Web of Things at W3C took meaningful steps forward, thanks to a diverse community of

00:00:12.019 --> 00:00:17.869 
contributors across multiple groups. We'd like to share a few of the highlights with you.

00:00:17.869 --> 00:00:24.661 
 Thanks to the members of the working group, new features have arrived in the Thing Description standard.

00:00:24.661 --> 00:00:34.683 
 The Thing Description gained Common Definitions , which let you declare 
shared protocol details once, instead of repeating them

00:00:34.683 --> 00:00:43.139 
 There is now also a binding registry, which opens the door for standardization work outside of W3C to contribute

00:00:43.139 --> 00:00:52.015 
- OPC UA Binding from OPC Foundation is one example of such a collaboration. Alongside it, new protocol bindings are

00:00:52.015 --> 00:00:55.222 
available and already in use in real-world systems.

00:00:55.222 --> 00:01:02.501 
 A new charter is in place as well, starting September 2026 and setting the direction for the years ahead

00:01:02.501 --> 00:01:08.522 
- so if you're interested in standardization for the Internet of Things, this is a good moment to get involved.

00:01:08.522 --> 00:01:23.425 
 The community group held six meetups and expanded their tutorial library, where you can get hands-on with the Web

00:01:23.425 --> 00:01:32.932 
of Things yourself.

00:01:32.932 --> 00:01:39.547 
This year, the Web Thing Protocol Community Group released the first specification of the Web Thing Protocol.

00:01:39.547 --> 00:01:46.934 
It is the protocol built natively on the Thing Description model to work on top of WebSockets. Implementations are already

00:01:46.934 --> 00:01:54.036 
underway, and it's heading toward the Working Group for standardization.

00:01:54.036 --> 00:01:59.077 
 The Web of Things is open source at heart, and it grows with every new contributor.

00:01:59.077 --> 00:02:06.323 
If you're interested in getting involved, a great place to start is the newly reworked website, which brings together all

00:02:06.323 --> 00:02:08.526 
relevant information in one place.

00:02:08.526 --> 00:02:11.577 
 From there you can also reach each group individually.

00:02:11.577 --> 00:02:21.879 
 If you'd rather meet the groups face to face, we'll be at TPAC in Dublin, and in January at

00:02:21.879 --> 00:02:29.786 
the WoT Open Day and Hands-on, in Shenzhen, China - two chances to experience the technology in action yourself.

00:02:29.786 --> 00:02:35.819 
You can click the links in the description to participate, or connect with the community on GitHub and Discord.

